## Recommended (not counted in the 9 pages, per the template) [T]
- Ethics statement (<= 1 page, end of main text before references) if the Code of Ethics is touched; a paragraph saying why none is needed is fine.
- Reproducibility statement (paragraph, end of main text before references) pointing to the theory proofs (appendix), code and results files; "should not itself describe details needed for reproducing".
- Uncounted-statement placement: the template says these statements "do not count" but also asks for them "at the end of the main text (before references)".
  To be safe against a stricter reading, the checker warns when the page count up to the References heading including the statements exceeds 9.
  Cheapest safe layout: main text + statements <= 9 pages, or place the AI use statement after the References.
- Default notation file `math_commands.tex` is optional ("can be disabled").

## Process facts (no action for the delivery) [S]
Abstract deadline 18 Sep 2026 and full-paper deadline 25 Sep 2026 (AOE) are past; reviews ~5 Nov 2026; conference 26-30 Apr 2027.
"Ready to submit" here therefore means conformance to everything above, not an actual OpenReview submission. Author-quota/reviewer-registration rules,
OpenReview profile requirements and the title-change freeze concern the submission form, not the files.

## Differences from the delivered NeurIPS 2026 paper that paper-repo must act on
1. Replace `neurips_2026.sty` and `checklist.tex` by `iclr2027_conference.sty` / `.bst`; **no NeurIPS-style checklist exists at ICLR** (delete it and its `\input`).
2. Title/author block: `\title{}` + the template's `\author` stays anonymous automatically; drop `\usepackage{neurips_2026}` options and the NeurIPS `ack` environment.
3. Citation style: author-year `natbib` with the supplied bst (`\bibliographystyle{iclr2027_conference}`); section heading style is set by the class (small caps), not bold sentence case; table/figure captions as in row 8.
4. Add the required **AI use statement** (row 4; the author team must state truthfully how the AI workers were used: ideas, derivations, code, experiments, writing) and, recommended, reproducibility and ethics statements.
5. Re-check that the main text fits 9 pages and that all figures read at the 5.5 in text width.
6. The cutoff for cited/used work moved from 2026-09-01 to **2026-10-18**; older citations remain valid.

## Supplementary size decision (venue-repo#9) [S for the rules, decision is ours]
Rules reported for ICLR 2027 [S]: code may be an anonymised .zip supplementary upload, **or** a link to an anonymous repository in the paper (both become public with the paper and reviews); after the discussion forums open, an anonymous link may instead be posted to reviewers and ACs only. No size cap is stated; treat it as unknown and plan for 50-100 MB.
Decision for the delivery (tree ~60 MB `code/`+`results/`, six raw-run assets 174.4 MiB listed with sha256 in `results/release_assets.json`):
1. Cap: unknown. The delivered tree must stand on its own: every number, figure and table must be reproducible from tracked `results/` summaries and `code/` without the assets; the assets are only the raw runs behind them.
2. Form: **(b)**. The assets are separate files with sha256, shipped beside the tree in the research delivery (release files), and `code/README.md` states the anonymous-archive route for an actual OpenReview submission: an anonymous archive link (anonymous hosting only, no author/institution/username in URL, path, metadata or commit history), or a post to reviewers/ACs after the forums open. If the supplementary upload field has a cap below the tree size, the tree itself must be trimmed to the smaller summary tree and the rest moved to the archive. (c) is the fallback, not the plan: "available on request" is weaker provenance than a checkable archive.
3. A link to the assets **must** pass row 5: no identifying host, owner, path or contents; archive internals (file names, metadata, logs, hostnames, git history) are grepped like the code. The `where` field of `release_assets.json` must say "separate files" without naming the Forgejo/Ibex host or a user.
Not an obstacle to the checker: it checks PDF + source only; the code anonymity grep is run on the assembled delivery.

## What cannot be established
- The verbatim ICLR 2027 Author Guidelines, AI Policy for Authors and Call for Papers (iclr.cc is unreachable from the sandbox); the [S] rows rest on search summaries.
  One third-party summary says the guidelines contradict themselves in two places (one reportedly on the submission page limit); the template's "strict upper limit of 9 pages" is the rule used here.
- Whether supplementary code has an upload size cap: **unknown** (re-checked 2026-10-18: ICLR 2027 Author Guidelines excerpts via search give no size; OpenReview's field is set per conference; NeurIPS 2026 uses 100 MB). Decision for the oversized raw-run assets: see "Supplementary size decision" below.
- The exact AI-statement wording of the submission form.
- Exact page layout of the statements relative to the 9 pages (see recommended-section note); page-count by text extraction is heuristic, confirm on the rendered pages.
- The actual OpenReview submission cannot be exercised; only conformance to the published rules is checkable.

